# Self-profile and application context

The API exposes a read-only caller-context endpoint. Cognito owns profile attributes:
name and email reads and optional display-name edits belong to Cognito user APIs called
by the UI. This split was selected during architecture review on 2026-11-19.
There is no REST profile write or password endpoint.

## GET /api/v1/me

Send the user's Cognito bearer access token. The endpoint returns:

```json
{
  "subject": "<validated Cognito sub>",
  "tenant": {
    "id": "<admitted tenant GUID>",
    "name": "<tenant directory display name>"
  },
  "roles": ["Users"]
}
```

- Subject comes from the SAME bearer token already accepted by JwtBearer. Both `sub`
  and Cognito `username` must be unambiguous non-empty scalar strings. The endpoint
  rejects machine access tokens, including tokens whose `sub` equals `client_id`.
- Tenant comes from the existing Core tenant context after active-directory admission.
  It never comes from a header, query selector, a default tenant or unverified UI claims.
  PlatformAdmin callers receive the same tenant admission checks as other callers.
- Roles are distinct, ordinally sorted values of the configured group claim used by API
  policies (`cognito:groups` by default). Case is preserved, as in policy evaluation.
  These values are token-time role claims, not a live group lookup or a complete list of
  permissions; endpoint policies still decide access.
- Identity profile attributes, email, user name, tokens and provider-edit capabilities are
  deliberately absent. No Cognito self-service scope, online provider call, IAM grant,
  database table or migration is needed for this endpoint.
- No subject or tenant selector is accepted. Extra query parameters and `X-Tenant-ID`
  cannot select another identity or tenant. There is no `/me/{id}` route.
- Only GET is exposed; write verbs return 405. Responses containing caller context use
  `Cache-Control: no-store`. No profile bodies or tokens are logged by this feature.

Anonymous/invalid bearer requests and non-user identities are rejected. A valid API key
does not grant user access, including in `Both` mode; an invalid bearer cannot fall back
to an API key. Missing/invalid/inactive tenant admission returns 403; directory
unavailability returns 503. Existing tenancy middleware can reject requests before
endpoint authorization. In API-key-only configuration the endpoint fails closed without
attempting to use an unregistered bearer handler.

Code belongs to `Platform/Profile`, with matching `Platform/Profile` tests. Simple
context projection needs no separate service layer or custom DI registration. MVC
discovers the controller through the existing application part. The built-in
`SelfProfile` policy name is reserved and cannot be replaced by configuration.

## Cognito profile reads and optional name edit (UI work is separate)

Recommended identity read: direct Cognito `GetUser`, authorized with the user's access
token. Optional update: direct `UpdateUserAttributes` with only standard `name`.
Both need `aws.cognito.signin.user.admin` and app-client attribute permissions; neither
requires AWS credentials or IAM permissions. A REST wrapper would require the same
permissions and adds no privilege bypass. Never reuse trusted administrator provisioning
or introduce `AdminUpdateUserAttributes`/`AdminGetUser` for self-profile.

For read-only managed-login integrations, `GET /oauth2/userInfo` is another supported
identity read. It needs `openid`; `email` and `profile` control returned attributes.
That path does not authorize attribute updates. `openid email profile` alone does not
authorize `GetUser` or `UpdateUserAttributes`.

Cognito `name` can be absent. Preserve that absence rather than saving an email/username
display fallback as the user's name. Identity reads must match their returned `sub`
to the current session subject; discard results after logout, account/tenant change or
a superseding read. Re-read the provider after an edit; token claims do not change in
place. The UI must continue using its supported session refresh and state-reset lifecycle.
The generated UI currently displays ID-token profile claims and access-token tenant/roles;
this API change does not implement or change UI profile flows.

## Operator prerequisites for self-service editing

Editing starts disabled in the planned UI flow. These are operator verification steps;
this print neither changes AWS configuration nor proves the deployed settings.
Audit write permissions BEFORE issuing the reserved scope even for `GetUser` reads:
a read-only UI does not make that token read-only. Existing ID-token display or an
appropriate read-only `userInfo` integration can avoid requesting that scope merely
to show identity attributes.

1. Use the existing public authorization-code + PKCE app client without a browser client
   secret. For managed login, BOTH allow and request `aws.cognito.signin.user.admin`
   before `GetUser` or `UpdateUserAttributes` is used. Adding configuration does not
   upgrade an existing token: use a new authorization flow rather than assuming refresh
   adds new scope grants. Do not add this scope merely to call the REST `/me` endpoint.
2. Configure readable attributes for `sub`, `name`, `email` and `email_verified`,
   with trusted tenant attributes readable only where needed. Attributes missing from
   the account or unreadable to the client remain unavailable.
3. Review EVERY untrusted/public app client's write attributes. Allow `name` only where
   the pool permits that policy; remove email, phone, tenant and other assignment writes
   if the product requires those attributes to be read-only. Hiding UI fields cannot
   constrain direct requests made with the same self-service token. The reserved scope
   authorizes other user self-service operations; it is not a name-only scope.
4. Pool-required attributes must be writable by an app client. Federated mappings can
   require write permissions and overwrite attributes at sign-in. If required email or
   external IdP ownership/mappings conflict with a name-only edit policy, keep editing
   disabled and resolve the architecture explicitly. A disabled button alone does not
   prove directly callable Cognito operations are disabled.
5. Keep `custom:tenant_id` outside EVERY untrusted client's write permissions and
   external IdP mappings, as required by [trusted provisioning](TrustedProvisioning.md).
   Tenant, roles, email assignment and verification status stay read-only in Settings.
   Existing PlatformAdmin provisioning permissions are separate and remain unchanged.
6. Before enabling name edit, verify denied direct attempts to update protected
   attributes, approved name updates, missing scopes, federated-account behavior and
   newly authorized sessions against an operator-controlled test environment. Local
   mocks cannot prove remote client settings. Do not change live AWS as part of template tests.

## Current token validation and freshness limits

`Platform/Infrastructure/Extensions/AuthenticationExtensions.cs` validates JWT signature,
issuer and lifetime. `Platform/Auth/CognitoTokenValidator.cs` additionally requires
`token_use=access` and an allowed `client_id`. Raw scalar `tenant_id` validation and
active-directory admission remain unchanged. The new endpoint validates user-subject
shape locally; this does not strengthen subject requirements on existing routes.

Unbound Cognito access tokens normally use `client_id` rather than `aud`. Resource-bound
tokens can additionally carry `aud` equal to a requested resource URL. Current REST
code disables audience validation for Cognito, including when `aud` is present.
It does not enforce resource binding. The legacy `Audience` setting can still act as
a client-ID fallback when `ClientIds` is empty; it is not a resource-audience setting.
Any resource-audience enforcement is a separate explicit auth change, preserving current
behavior until reviewed.

JWT lifetime validation uses the token library's default clock skew; it is not explicitly
configured. Offline signature/lifetime validation cannot detect revocation before expiry.
Cognito token-authorized user APIs can reject revoked tokens, but direct UI provider reads
do not turn other API calls into online revocation checks. Tenant directory admission also
has its existing cache lifetime. Roles and tenant claims remain token-time state until
refresh/re-authorization; directory status is observed according to admission/cache policy.

Viewing this context has no new recent-login requirement. Tightening clock skew or adding
recent interactive authentication must be reviewed separately for sensitive actions.
A recent `iat` can result from refresh and does not prove recent interactive login;
`auth_time`, with an agreed maximum age and reauthentication behavior, would be needed
for that guarantee. Neither refresh, `GetUser` nor an existing login cookie proves that
the user just entered credentials.

## Passwords and verification boundary

Password flow remains a separate decision. No REST password payload, proxy, storage or
logging is permitted, and there is no invented managed-login account/settings URL.
Cognito documents a token-authorized `ChangePassword` operation; suitability depends on
local-password, federated and passwordless accounts and an approved UI/recovery design.
This feature does not enable that flow.

Generated profile tests use real locally signed JWTs, MVC, the production authentication
registration and Core admission middleware, with mocked directory/secret dependencies.
They check response fields, no-store, custom role mapping, user/machine identity shape,
bearer-only access, tenant selection resistance, directory denials and no write routes.
Existing Cognito/JWT, tenant-isolation, architecture and model-snapshot tests remain
applicable. No cloud calls or migrations are part of verification.

